    "question": {
      "math_academy_id": "q-88516",
      "sequence_position": 2,
      "problem": "Determine which of the following is the graph of $x=(2y+1)^{2}-6\\,$ by completing the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $$ | $$ | $$ | $$ |",
      "worked_solution": "To graph this curve, we can start by calculating the value of $x$ for various values of $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y=-\\frac{1}{2} & \\,⟶\\,x={[2\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2})+1]}^{2}-6=-6 \\\\ y=0 & \\,⟶\\,x=(2\\cdot 0+1)^{2}-6=-5 \\\\ y=1 & \\,⟶\\,x=(2\\cdot 1+1)^{2}-6=3 \\\\ y=\\frac{3}{2} & \\,⟶\\,x={[2\\cdot (\\frac{3}{2})+1]}^{2}-6=10\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can summarize these results in the following table:\n\n| **$y$** | $-\\frac{1}{2}$ | $0$ | $1$ | $\\frac{3}{2}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $-6$ | $-5$ | $3$ | $10$ |\n\nSo, we have the following points:\n\n$$\n(-6,-\\frac{1}{2}),\\,(-5,0),\\,(3,1),\\,(10,\\frac{3}{2}).\n$$\n\nIf we plot these points and connect them with a smooth curve, we get the following graph:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14240562/assets/ded8b752a5acdd94b4e73a4cf7b301ed0d616b0cfd35896b5c9e5275fc8ca2ad.png)",
